# The example with run E's slots (slot.trace) and its handle state (HMEM, HTABLE,
# HANDLE, HOBJ and HPTR lines; bergdb item 009, step 4c). After T(1), 3 and 5 are in
# D, with canonical objects in their live handles, and 3's pointer for 5 holds 5's.
# After T(2), 5's handle was evicted: dead, with the written position R(2), which
# 3's pointer, naming R(0), is written with (the record rule). After T(3), a read
# went through that pointer: it raised the reference to R(2), and put 5's new live
# handle, with a snapshot of R(2), into it. Expected: C-H passes, TRACECHECK OK.
TRACE 2
CONFIG example
TX 0
NEW 0 9 I
NEW 3 8 I
NEW 5 1 I
ADD 0 0 T 3
ADD 3 0 R 5
REC 0 0 0 field
VER 3 I
H 0 R 5
F 5 0
VER 5 I
DIRTY
END 0
TX 1
NEW 7 0 D
ADD 7 1 V 123
ADD 5 1 K 40 7
REC 1 0 0 field
VER 7 D
H 1 V 123
GD 3 5
DIRTY 3 1 5 1
LIVE 5 1 7
LIVE 0 1 3
SLOT 3 5 0 canon
SLOT 5 7 1 mem
HMEM 0 0
HTABLE 3 1
HTABLE 5 2
HANDLE 1 3 live 1 -
HANDLE 2 5 live 2 -
HOBJ 1 3 canon 0 1
HOBJ 2 5 canon 0 1
HPTR 1 5 0 2
END 1
TX 2
REC 2 1 2 field
VER 3 I
H 0 R 5
F 5 2
VER 5 I
H 1 K 40 7
F 7 1
GD
DIRTY
SLOT 3 5 2 stream
HMEM 0 0
HTABLE 3 1
HANDLE 1 3 live 1 -
HANDLE 2 5 dead - 2
HOBJ 1 3 canon 2 1
HPTR 1 5 0 2
END 2
TX 3
REC 3 2 2 field
GD
DIRTY
SLOT 3 5 2 mem
HMEM 0 0
HTABLE 3 1
HTABLE 5 3
HANDLE 1 3 live 1 -
HANDLE 3 5 live 2 -
HOBJ 1 3 canon 2 1
HOBJ 2 5 snap 2 1
HPTR 1 5 2 3
END 3
DONE 3
